<section data-testid="about" id="problem" class="py-10 lg:py-14">
	<p class="mb-4 font-monospace text-base font-bold text-orange lg:text-lg">01 — The Problem</p>
	<h2 class="mb-6 max-w-2xl font-monospace text-2xl text-black dark:text-white lg:text-4xl">
		Moving to a new city means eating alone — a lot.
	</h2>

	<div class="rounded-3xl bg-gray-50 p-7 dark:bg-gray-900 lg:p-10">
		<div class="grid gap-8 lg:grid-cols-2 lg:gap-14">
			<p class="text-sm leading-loose text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
				Communal dishes like hotpot and dim sum lose their appeal without company. Existing platforms
				like Meetup handle large events, but nothing exists for the simple, recurring need: finding
				one to three people to share a meal with. We set out to design a mobile app that makes it
				effortless to find a compatible dining companion — not just someone who likes the same
				cuisine, but someone you can actually talk to.
			</p>
			<blockquote class="border-l-4 border-orange pl-5">
				<p class="mb-2 text-lg font-semibold italic leading-snug text-black dark:text-white">
					"People don't want a dining companion who likes the same food. They want someone they can
					actually talk to."
				</p>
				<cite class="text-xs font-bold not-italic uppercase tracking-widest text-orange">
					Core insight from user research
				</cite>
			</blockquote>
		</div>
	</div>
</section>
